Price is the observed exchange amount; value estimates future economic benefits under assumptions.|A share trades at ₹80 while an analyst estimates ₹100 with uncertainty.|The value estimate guarantees price will reach ₹100?|No|Yes|Only after publication
Intrinsic valuation estimates discounted fundamentals; relative valuation uses comparable market pricing.|DCF values cash flows; a peer P/E applies market pricing to earnings.|A peer multiple is independent of market sentiment?|No|Yes|Only for profitable firms
Enterprise value prices operating claims across capital providers; equity value belongs to shareholders.|Operating value ₹100 + excess cash ₹10 − debt ₹30 gives equity ₹80.|Equity value here is…?|₹80|₹100|₹140
The enterprise-to-equity bridge adjusts operating value for non-operating assets and other claims consistently.|EV ₹200 + excess cash ₹20 − debt ₹70 − other claims ₹10 gives equity ₹140.|Include the same cash twice?|No|Yes|Only if unrestricted
FCFF is cash for all capital providers; FCFE is equity cash after financing flows.|FCFE = FCFF − after-tax interest + net borrowing, under consistent definitions.|FCFE normally uses which discount rate?|Cost of equity|WACC unchanged|Historical cost of debt
Dividend discount models value expected owner distributions at the equity required return.|Next dividend ₹5, return 10%, growth 2% gives value ₹62.50.|A zero current dividend necessarily means zero equity value?|No|Yes|Only for startups
DCF discounts expected cash flows and a justified terminal value using consistent rates.|Next-year FCFF ₹10 at 10% with zero perpetual growth gives operating value ₹100.|Higher WACC with unchanged cash lowers…?|Present value|Past revenue|Share count
APV values unlevered operations separately from financing benefits and costs.|Operating value ₹100 + shield PV ₹10 − financing costs ₹2 gives ₹108.|APV is…?|₹108|₹112|₹100
Residual income values equity earnings above its capital charge; economic profit uses operating capital consistently.|Book equity ₹100, profit ₹15, required equity return 12%: residual income ₹3.|Positive accounting profit always means positive residual income?|No|Yes|Only with no dividends
Forecast horizons reflect when business economics approach a sustainable state; advantage periods reflect excess-return persistence.|A patent expires after five years, changing competitive margins.|Should today's excess margin be extrapolated forever automatically?|No|Yes|Only if recent growth is high
Growth requires reinvestment and productive returns; growth alone can destroy value.|Reinvestment rate 40% × ROIC 15% implies 6% operating growth in a simplified steady-state model.|With ROIC below WACC, more such growth tends to…?|Destroy value|Create value automatically|Eliminate capital needs
Terminal growth models cash beyond explicit forecasts; sustainable growth must be below the discount rate.|FCFF next year ₹12, WACC 10%, growth 4% gives terminal value ₹200 at the terminal date.|Discount terminal value back to today?|Yes|No|Only if positive
Exit multiples estimate terminal value from a future financial metric and a justified multiple.|Terminal EBITDA ₹20 × EV/EBITDA 8 gives terminal EV ₹160.|This method is purely independent of market assumptions?|No|Yes|Only for long forecasts
Mid-year discounting approximates within-year cash timing; stub periods account for partial years.|A half-year cash flow is discounted for its actual assumed timing.|Treat all partial-year cash as a full year without adjustment?|No|Yes|Only for quarterly models
Excess cash and non-operating assets add value outside operating cash flows, subject to accessibility and double-counting checks.|Operating DCF excludes a separately valued investment worth ₹20.|Add that investment once in the bridge?|Yes|No|Only if sold
Debt, leases, pension deficits, and NCI are claims requiring consistent treatment with forecast cash and enterprise scope.|Consolidated cash flows include 100% of a subsidiary with outside ownership.|The bridge should consider…?|Non-controlling interests|Only parent debt|Only parent dividends
Dilutive awards redistribute equity claims; options and restricted stock need method-consistent treatment.|Equity ₹100 over 10 shares gives ₹10 before relevant dilution adjustments.|Ignoring material employee options can overstate…?|Value per existing share|Enterprise revenue|Gross margin
Sensitivities vary inputs; scenarios combine assumptions; reverse DCF infers expectations embedded in price.|A high price requires unusually long excess-return persistence in a reverse model.|Reverse DCF establishes those expectations will occur?|No|Yes|Only in efficient markets
Valuation ranges represent uncertainty in drivers, rates, and outcomes rather than false precision.|Plausible cases produce ₹80, ₹100, and ₹130 values.|Best presentation includes…?|Assumptions and a justified range|Only ₹100.0000|Only the highest case
